<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-expectation-moments">
<title>Expectation and Moments</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Random variables, discrete and continuous distributions, joint
distributions, probability mass functions, probability density
functions, infinite series, definite and improper integrals, and basic
algebra.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>define expected value for discrete and continuous random
          variables;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interpret expectation as a probability-weighted average;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>determine whether an expectation exists;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute expectations using PMFs and PDFs;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>apply the law of the unconscious statistician;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use linearity of expectation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute expectations using indicator variables;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define raw moments and central moments;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define variance and standard deviation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use equivalent formulas for variance;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand the effects of linear transformations on mean and
          variance;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute means and variances of standard probability
          distributions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define covariance;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>derive covariance identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define correlation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interpret the sign and magnitude of correlation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish independence, uncorrelatedness, and perfect linear
          dependence;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute variance of sums of random variables;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand covariance matrices;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define conditional expectation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use the law of total expectation;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use the law of total variance;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define higher moments, skewness, and kurtosis;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand factorial moments;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize situations where moments fail to exist;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>apply moment inequalities such as Markov and Chebyshev;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>connect moments with transforms, limit theorems, statistics,
          and stochastic processes.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-what-is-expected-value">
<title>What Is Expected Value?</title>
<p>The expected value of a random variable is a probability-weighted
average of its possible values.</p>

<p>It is often interpreted as the long-run average value that would emerge
if the same probabilistic experiment were repeated many times.</p>

<p>The expected value of <m>X</m> is written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X].
}</md>
</p>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}</md>
</p>

<p>is read <q>expectation.</q></p>

<p>One also commonly writes</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mu_X
=
\mathbb{E}[X].
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-discrete-expectation">
<title>Expected Value of a Discrete Random Variable</title>
<definition>
<title>Expected Value: Discrete Case</title>
<statement>
<p>If <m>X</m> is discrete with PMF</p>

<p>
<md>p_X(x)=P(X=x),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\sum_x
x\,p_X(x),
}</md>
</p>

<p>provided the sum is well-defined.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Each possible value is multiplied by its probability.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-expected-die">
<title>Worked Example: Expected Die Result</title>
<example>
<title>Mean of a Fair Die</title>
<p>Let <m>X</m> be the result of a fair six-sided die.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>P(X=x)=\frac16,
\qquad
x=1,\ldots,6.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\sum_{x=1}^{6}
x\left(\frac16\right).</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\frac{1+2+3+4+5+6}{6}.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\frac72.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-expectation-not-possible-value">
<title>Expectation Need Not Be a Possible Value</title>
<p>The expected value does not need to belong to the support.</p>

<p>For a fair die,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
=
3.5,</md>
</p>

<p>even though no roll can equal <m>3.5</m>.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{expected value}
\neq
\text{necessarily an attainable outcome}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>It is a weighted center of the distribution.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-continuous-expectation">
<title>Expected Value of a Continuous Random Variable</title>
<definition>
<title>Expected Value: Continuous Case</title>
<statement>
<p>If <m>X</m> has probability density <m>f_X</m>, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
x f_X(x)\,dx,
}</md>
</p>

<p>provided the integral exists.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The integral is the continuous analogue of the weighted sum used in the
discrete case.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-uniform-mean">
<title>Worked Example: Uniform Mean</title>
<example>
<title>Mean of a Uniform Distribution</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(a,b).</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>f_X(x)
=
\frac1{b-a}</md>
</p>

<p>for</p>

<p>
<md>a\leq x\leq b.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\int_a^b
x\frac1{b-a}\,dx.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>=
\frac1{b-a}
\left[
\frac{x^2}{2}
\right]_a^b.</md>
</p>

<p>Hence,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\frac{a+b}{2}.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-expectation-existence">
<title>Existence of Expectation</title>
<p>Expectation is not automatically finite.</p>

<p>A useful sufficient condition is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[|X|]&lt;\infty.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For a discrete variable,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[|X|]
=
\sum_x
|x|p_X(x).</md>
</p>

<p>For a continuous variable,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[|X|]
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
|x|f_X(x)\,dx.</md>
</p>

<p>If this quantity is finite, then <m>\mathbb{E}[X]</m> exists as a finite
real number.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-expectation-function-rv">
<title>Expectation of a Function</title>
<p>Often one wants</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[g(X)]</md>
</p>

<p>for some function <m>g</m>.</p>

<p>It is not necessary to first find the distribution of <m>g(X)</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-lotus">
<title>Law of the Unconscious Statistician</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Law of the Unconscious Statistician]
If <m>X</m> is discrete, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[g(X)]
=
\sum_x
g(x)p_X(x).
}</md>
</p>

<p>If <m>X</m> is continuous with density <m>f_X</m>, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[g(X)]
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
g(x)f_X(x)\,dx.
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>This result is often abbreviated informally as LOTUS.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-expected-square">
<title>Worked Example: Expected Square</title>
<example>
<title>Square of a Fair Die</title>
<p>Let <m>X</m> be a fair die result.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X^2]
=
\sum_{x=1}^{6}
x^2\frac16.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X^2]
=
\frac{
1^2+2^2+3^2+4^2+5^2+6^2
}{6}.</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>1+4+9+16+25+36=91,</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X^2]
=
\frac{91}{6}.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-linearity-expectation">
<title>Linearity of Expectation</title>
<p>One of the most useful facts in probability is linearity of
expectation.</p>

<p>\begin{theorem}[Linearity of Expectation]
For random variables <m>X_1,\ldots,X_n</m> with finite expectations and
constants <m>a_1,\ldots,a_n,b</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}
\left[
b+\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_iX_i
\right]
=
b+\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i\mathbb{E}[X_i].
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Independence is not required.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-two-variable-linearity">
<title>Two-Variable Linearity</title>
<p>In particular,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X+Y]
=
\mathbb{E}[X]
+
\mathbb{E}[Y].
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[aX+b]
=
a\mathbb{E}[X]+b.
}</md>
</p>

<p>These formulas hold regardless of dependence.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-linearity-cost">
<title>Worked Example: Total Cost</title>
<example>
<title>Linear Cost Transformation</title>
<p>Suppose <m>X</m> is a random number of units used and</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]=40.</md>
</p>

<p>A cost is defined by</p>

<p>
<md>C=5X+12.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[C]
=
5\mathbb{E}[X]+12.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[C]
=
212.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-indicator-expectation">
<title>Indicators and Expectation</title>
<p>For an event <m>A</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{1}_A
=
\begin{cases}
1,&amp;A\text{ occurs},\\
0,&amp;A\text{ does not occur}.
\end{cases}</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_A]
=
1\cdot P(A)
+
0\cdot P(A^c).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}_A]
=
P(A).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is one of the most useful identities in combinatorial probability.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-counting-with-indicators">
<title>Counting with Indicators</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
\mathbf{1}_{A_i}.</md>
</p>

<p>Then <m>X</m> counts the number of events <m>A_i</m> that occur.</p>

<p>By linearity,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
P(A_i).
}</md>
</p>

<p>No independence is required.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-fixed-points-expectation">
<title>Worked Example: Fixed Points of a Random Permutation</title>
<example>
<title>Expected Number of Fixed Points</title>
<p>Choose a permutation of</p>

<p>
<md>\{1,\ldots,n\}</md>
</p>

<p>uniformly at random.</p>

<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>I_i
=
\mathbf{1}_{\{\pi(i)=i\}}.</md>
</p>

<p>Then the number of fixed points is</p>

<p>
<md>X
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}I_i.</md>
</p>

<p>For each <m>i</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>P(\pi(i)=i)
=
\frac1n.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
\frac1n.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]=1.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-tail-sum-formula">
<title>Tail-Sum Formula</title>
<p>For a nonnegative integer-valued random variable,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}
P(X\geq k).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Equivalently,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
P(X&gt;k).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is called the tail-sum formula.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-continuous-tail-formula">
<title>Continuous Tail Formula</title>
<p>For a nonnegative continuous random variable,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\int_0^\infty
P(X&gt;x)\,dx.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>P(X&gt;x)=S_X(x),</md>
</p>

<p>we may write</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\int_0^\infty
S_X(x)\,dx.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-exponential-tail-mean">
<title>Worked Example: Exponential Mean from the Tail</title>
<example>
<title>Mean Using the Survival Function</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Exp}(\lambda).</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>P(X&gt;x)
=
e^{-\lambda x}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\int_0^\infty
e^{-\lambda x}\,dx.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\frac1\lambda.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-moments">
<title>Moments</title>
<p>Moments summarize important features of a probability distribution.</p>

<definition>
<title>Raw Moment</title>
<statement>
<p>The <m>k</m>-th raw moment of <m>X</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mu_k'
=
\mathbb{E}[X^k],
}</md>
</p>

<p>provided the expectation exists.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The first raw moment is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mu_1'
=
\mathbb{E}[X].
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-central-moments">
<title>Central Moments</title>
<definition>
<title>Central Moment</title>
<statement>
<p>The <m>k</m>-th central moment of <m>X</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mu_k
=
\mathbb{E}
\left[
(X-\mu)^k
\right],
}</md>
</p>

<p>where</p>

<p>
<md>\mu=\mathbb{E}[X].</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The first central moment is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mu_1=0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The second central moment is the variance.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-variance">
<title>Variance</title>
<definition>
<title>Variance</title>
<statement>
<p>The variance of <m>X</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\mathbb{E}
\left[
(X-\mathbb{E}[X])^2
\right].
}</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Variance measures average squared deviation from the mean.</p>

<p>It is always nonnegative.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-variance-nonnegative">
<title>Variance Is Nonnegative</title>
<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>(X-\mathbb{E}[X])^2
\geq0,</md>
</p>

<p>its expectation also satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)\geq0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Moreover,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X)=0</md>
</p>

<p>if and only if <m>X</m> is constant almost surely.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-variance-computational-formula">
<title>Computational Formula for Variance</title>
<p>Expand:</p>

<p>
<md>(X-\mu)^2
=
X^2-2\mu X+\mu^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Taking expectations,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\mathbb{E}[X^2]
-
2\mu\mathbb{E}[X]
+
\mu^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>\mu=\mathbb{E}[X],</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\mathbb{E}[X^2]
-
(\mathbb{E}[X])^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-die-variance">
<title>Worked Example: Variance of a Fair Die</title>
<example>
<title>Variance of a Fair Die</title>
<p>For a fair die,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\frac72</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X^2]
=
\frac{91}{6}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\frac{91}{6}
-
\left(
\frac72
\right)^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>=
\frac{182}{12}
-
\frac{147}{12}.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\frac{35}{12}.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-standard-deviation">
<title>Standard Deviation</title>
<definition>
<title>Standard Deviation</title>
<statement>
<p>The standard deviation of <m>X</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sigma_X
=
\sqrt{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
}.
}</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Variance has squared units.</p>

<p>Standard deviation has the same units as <m>X</m>.</p>

<p>This often makes standard deviation easier to interpret.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-translation-variance">
<title>Effect of Translation on Variance</title>
<p>Adding a constant does not change spread.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X+b)
=
\operatorname{Var}(X).
}</md>
</p>

<p>The entire distribution shifts, but distances from its mean remain the
same.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-scaling-variance">
<title>Effect of Scaling on Variance</title>
<p>For a constant <m>a</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(aX)
=
a^2\operatorname{Var}(X).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(aX+b)
=
a^2\operatorname{Var}(X).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Correspondingly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sigma_{aX+b}
=
|a|\sigma_X.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-linear-variance">
<title>Worked Example: Linear Transformation</title>
<example>
<title>Transforming Mean and Variance</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]=8</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X)=9.</md>
</p>

<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>Y=4X-3.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[Y]
=
4(8)-3
=
29.</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(Y)
=
4^2(9).</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[Y]=29,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(Y)=144.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-bernoulli-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Bernoulli</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
=
0(1-p)+1(p).</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]=p.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>X^2=X</md>
</p>

<p>for <m>X\in\{0,1\}</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X^2]=p.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
p(1-p).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-binomial-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Binomial</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Bin}(n,p),</md>
</p>

<p>write</p>

<p>
<md>X
=
X_1+\cdots+X_n</md>
</p>

<p>where the <m>X_i</m> are independent Bernoulli<m>(p)</m> variables.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
\mathbb{E}[X_i].</md>
</p>

<p>Hence,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]=np.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Using independence,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
np(1-p).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-geometric-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Geometric</title>
<p>Under the convention that</p>

<p>
<md>X
=
\text{number of trials until first success},</md>
</p>

<p>if</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Geom}(p),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\frac1p.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The variance is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\frac{1-p}{p^2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-negative-binomial-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Negative Binomial</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X
\sim
\operatorname{NegBin}(r,p)</md>
</p>

<p>counts the number of trials needed to obtain the <m>r</m>-th success, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\frac{r}{p}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\frac{r(1-p)}{p^2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This follows by interpreting <m>X</m> as a sum of <m>r</m> independent
geometric waiting times.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-hypergeometric-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Hypergeometric</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X
\sim
\operatorname{Hypergeom}(N,K,n),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
n\frac KN.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The variance is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
n
\frac KN
\left(
1-\frac KN
\right)
\frac{N-n}{N-1}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The factor</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{N-n}{N-1}
}</md>
</p>

<p>is called the finite population correction.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-poisson-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Poisson</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\lambda.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\lambda.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the Poisson distribution has equal mean and variance.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-poisson-standard-deviation">
<title>Worked Example: Poisson Standard Deviation</title>
<example>
<title>Poisson Spread</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(16).</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]=16</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X)=16.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sigma_X=4.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-discrete-uniform-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Discrete Uniform</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X</md>
</p>

<p>is uniformly distributed on</p>

<p>
<md>\{1,2,\ldots,n\},</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\frac{n+1}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its variance is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\frac{n^2-1}{12}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-continuous-uniform-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Continuous Uniform</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(a,b),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\frac{a+b}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The variance is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\frac{(b-a)^2}{12}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-exponential-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Exponential</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Exp}(\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\frac1\lambda.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The variance is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\frac1{\lambda^2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sigma_X
=
\frac1\lambda.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-gamma-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Gamma</title>
<p>Using the shape-rate parameterization,</p>

<p>
<md>X
\sim
\operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha,\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>we have</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\frac{\alpha}{\lambda}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The variance is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\frac{\alpha}{\lambda^2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-normal-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Normal</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim N(\mu,\sigma^2),</md>
</p>

<p>then by definition of the parameters,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\mu
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\sigma^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the normal notation directly records its mean and variance.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-beta-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Beta</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Beta}(\alpha,\beta),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The variance is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\frac{
\alpha\beta
}{
(\alpha+\beta)^2
(\alpha+\beta+1)
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-chi-square-mean-variance">
<title>Mean and Variance of Chi-Square</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\chi^2_\nu,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]=\nu
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)=2\nu.
}</md>
</p>

<p>These formulas follow from the gamma representation</p>

<p>
<md>\chi^2_\nu
=
\operatorname{Gamma}
\left(
\frac{\nu}{2},
\frac12
\right).</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-mean-of-sum">
<title>Mean of a Sum</title>
<p>For any finite collection of random variables with finite expectations,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}
\left[
\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_i
\right]
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
\mathbb{E}[X_i].
}</md>
</p>

<p>This requires no independence.</p>

<p>Variance behaves differently.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-covariance">
<title>Covariance</title>
<definition>
<title>Covariance</title>
<statement>
<p>For random variables <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> with appropriate finite moments,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)
=
\mathbb{E}
\left[
(X-\mathbb{E}[X])
(Y-\mathbb{E}[Y])
\right].
}</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Covariance describes linear co-movement.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-covariance-interpretation">
<title>Interpretation of Covariance</title>
<p>If <m>X</m> tends to be above its mean when <m>Y</m> is also above its mean,
covariance tends to be positive.</p>

<p>If <m>X</m> tends to be above its mean when <m>Y</m> is below its mean,
covariance tends to be negative.</p>

<p>If there is little linear co-movement, covariance may be near zero.</p>

<p>Thus:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)&gt;0
}</md>
</p>

<p>suggests positive linear association,</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)&lt;0
}</md>
</p>

<p>suggests negative linear association.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-covariance-computational">
<title>Computational Formula for Covariance</title>
<p>Expanding the definition gives</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)
=
\mathbb{E}[XY]
-
\mathbb{E}[X]\mathbb{E}[Y].
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is usually the most convenient computational formula.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-variance-self-covariance">
<title>Variance as Self-Covariance</title>
<p>Setting</p>

<p>
<md>Y=X,</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(X,X)
=
\operatorname{Var}(X).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus variance is a special case of covariance.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-covariance-symmetry">
<title>Symmetry of Covariance</title>
<p>Because multiplication is commutative,</p>

<p>
<md>(X-\mu_X)(Y-\mu_Y)
=
(Y-\mu_Y)(X-\mu_X).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)
=
\operatorname{Cov}(Y,X).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-covariance-constants">
<title>Covariance with Constants</title>
<p>For any constant <m>c</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(X,c)=0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>A constant does not vary, so it cannot co-vary with <m>X</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-scaling-covariance">
<title>Scaling Covariance</title>
<p>For constants <m>a,b,c,d</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(aX+b,cY+d)
=
ac\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Translations do not affect covariance.</p>

<p>Scalings multiply covariance.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-covariance-sums">
<title>Covariance of Sums</title>
<p>Covariance is bilinear.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(X+Y,Z)
=
\operatorname{Cov}(X,Z)
+
\operatorname{Cov}(Y,Z).
}</md>
</p>

<p>More generally,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}
\left(
\sum_i a_iX_i,
\sum_j b_jY_j
\right)
=
\sum_i\sum_j
a_ib_j
\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,Y_j).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-variance-sum">
<title>Variance of a Sum</title>
<p>Using covariance,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X+Y)
=
\operatorname{Var}(X)
+
\operatorname{Var}(Y)
+
2\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y).</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X+Y)
=
\operatorname{Var}(X)
+
\operatorname{Var}(Y)
+
2\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-variance-difference">
<title>Variance of a Difference</title>
<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X-Y)
=
\operatorname{Var}(X)
+
\operatorname{Var}(Y)
-
2\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y).
}</md>
</p>

<p>The sign changes because <m>Y</m> is multiplied by <m>-1</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-variance-linear-combination">
<title>Variance of a General Linear Combination</title>
<p>For constants <m>a_1,\ldots,a_n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}
\left(
\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_iX_i
\right)
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
a_i^2
\operatorname{Var}(X_i)
+
2
\sum_{i&lt;j}
a_ia_j
\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is the general variance expansion.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-variance-independent-sums">
<title>Variance of Independent Sums</title>
<p>If <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are independent and have finite second moments, then</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=0.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X+Y)
=
\operatorname{Var}(X)
+
\operatorname{Var}(Y).
}</md>
</p>

<p>For independent <m>X_1,\ldots,X_n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}
\left(
\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_i
\right)
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
\operatorname{Var}(X_i).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-independence-zero-covariance">
<title>Independence Implies Zero Covariance</title>
<p>If <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are independent, then</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[XY]
=
\mathbb{E}[X]
\mathbb{E}[Y].</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{independence}
\Longrightarrow
\text{uncorrelatedness}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The converse does not generally hold.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-uncorrelated-dependent">
<title>Worked Example: Uncorrelated but Dependent</title>
<example>
<title>A Nonlinear Dependence</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Uniform}(-1,1)</md>
</p>

<p>and define</p>

<p>
<md>Y=X^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Then <m>Y</m> is completely determined by <m>X</m>, so <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are
dependent.</p>

<p>By symmetry,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]=0.</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[XY]
=
\mathbb{E}[X^3]
=
0.</md>
</p>

<p>Hence,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)
=
0.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
X\text{ and }Y\text{ are dependent but uncorrelated}.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-correlation">
<title>Correlation</title>
<definition>
<title>Correlation</title>
<statement>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\sigma_X&gt;0</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\sigma_Y&gt;0,</md>
</p>

<p>the correlation coefficient is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\rho_{X,Y}
=
\frac{
\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)
}{
\sigma_X\sigma_Y
}.
}</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The Greek letter</p>

<p>
<md>\rho</md>
</p>

<p>is called <q>rho.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-correlation-bounds-full">
<title>Correlation Bounds</title>
<p>By the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
-1
\leq
\rho_{X,Y}
\leq
1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus correlation is standardized to a dimensionless scale.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-extreme-correlations">
<title>Meaning of Extreme Correlations</title>
<p>Under suitable nondegeneracy conditions,</p>

<p>
<md>\rho_{X,Y}=1</md>
</p>

<p>means there is a positive exact linear relationship</p>

<p>
<md>Y=aX+b,
\qquad
a&gt;0.</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\rho_{X,Y}=-1</md>
</p>

<p>corresponds to</p>

<p>
<md>Y=aX+b,
\qquad
a&lt;0.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus perfect correlation represents exact linear dependence.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-correlation-scale">
<title>Correlation Is Invariant to Positive Rescaling</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>U=aX+b</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>V=cY+d</md>
</p>

<p>with</p>

<p>
<md>a&gt;0,
\qquad
c&gt;0.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\rho_{U,V}
=
\rho_{X,Y}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Changing measurement units therefore does not alter correlation.</p>

<p>If one scale factor is negative, the sign of correlation reverses.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-correlation">
<title>Worked Example: Correlation from Covariance</title>
<example>
<title>Standardizing Covariance</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=6,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\sigma_X=2,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\sigma_Y=5.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\rho_{X,Y}
=
\frac6{(2)(5)}.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\rho_{X,Y}=0.6.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-covariance-matrix">
<title>Covariance Matrix</title>
<p>For a random vector</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{X}
=
(X_1,\ldots,X_n)^T,</md>
</p>

<p>define the covariance matrix</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\Sigma
=
\operatorname{Cov}(\mathbf{X}).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its entries are</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\Sigma_{ij}
=
\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\Sigma
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\operatorname{Var}(X_1)
&amp;
\operatorname{Cov}(X_1,X_2)
&amp;
\cdots
\\
\operatorname{Cov}(X_2,X_1)
&amp;
\operatorname{Var}(X_2)
&amp;
\cdots
\\
\vdots
&amp;
\vdots
&amp;
\ddots
\end{pmatrix}.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-covariance-matrix-properties">
<title>Properties of Covariance Matrices</title>
<p>A covariance matrix is symmetric:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\Sigma^T=\Sigma.
}</md>
</p>

<p>It is also positive semidefinite.</p>

<p>For every vector <m>a</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
a^T\Sigma a\geq0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Indeed,</p>

<p>
<md>a^T\Sigma a
=
\operatorname{Var}(a^T\mathbf{X}).</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-variance-matrix-form">
<title>Variance of a Linear Combination in Matrix Form</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>Y=a^T\mathbf{X}.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(Y)
=
a^T\Sigma a.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This compact formula is fundamental in multivariate statistics,
portfolio theory, and optimization.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-expectation-products">
<title>Expectation of Products</title>
<p>In general,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[XY]
\neq
\mathbb{E}[X]\mathbb{E}[Y].
}</md>
</p>

<p>The equality holds when <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are independent under suitable
integrability conditions.</p>

<p>Thus,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
X\perp Y
\Longrightarrow
\mathbb{E}[g(X)h(Y)]
=
\mathbb{E}[g(X)]
\mathbb{E}[h(Y)]
}</md>
</p>

<p>whenever the relevant expectations exist.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-joint-expectation-discrete">
<title>Expectation with Joint PMFs</title>
<p>For discrete <m>X,Y</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[g(X,Y)]
=
\sum_x\sum_y
g(x,y)
p_{X,Y}(x,y).
}</md>
</p>

<p>In particular,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[XY]
=
\sum_x\sum_y
xy
p_{X,Y}(x,y).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-joint-expectation-continuous">
<title>Expectation with Joint Densities</title>
<p>For continuous <m>X,Y</m> with joint density,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[g(X,Y)]
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
g(x,y)
f_{X,Y}(x,y)
\,dy\,dx.
}</md>
</p>

<p>In particular,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[XY]
=
\iint
xyf_{X,Y}(x,y)
\,dx\,dy.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-conditional-expectation-discrete">
<title>Conditional Expectation: Discrete Case</title>
<p>Suppose <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are discrete.</p>

<p>For a value <m>y</m> with</p>

<p>
<md>P(Y=y)&gt;0,</md>
</p>

<p>define</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y=y]
=
\sum_x
x
P(X=x\mid Y=y).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is the expected value of the conditional distribution of <m>X</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-conditional-expectation-continuous">
<title>Conditional Expectation: Continuous Case</title>
<p>If a conditional density exists,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y=y]
=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
x
f_{X\mid Y}(x\mid y)
\,dx.
}</md>
</p>

<p>As <m>y</m> varies, this produces a function of <m>y</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-conditional-expectation-rv">
<title>Conditional Expectation as a Random Variable</title>
<p>The expression</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y]
}</md>
</p>

<p>is itself a random variable.</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>m(y)
=
\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y=y],</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y]
=
m(Y).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus conditional expectation is a function of the conditioning random
variable.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-law-total-expectation">
<title>Law of Total Expectation</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Law of Total Expectation]
If the relevant expectations exist, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\mathbb{E}
\left[
\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y]
\right].
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>This is also called the tower property in a more general setting.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-discrete-total-expectation">
<title>Discrete Total Expectation Formula</title>
<p>If <m>Y</m> is discrete,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\sum_y
\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y=y]
P(Y=y).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the unconditional mean is a weighted average of conditional means.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-total-expectation">
<title>Worked Example: Total Expectation</title>
<example>
<title>Two Types of Customers</title>
<p>Suppose a customer is type <m>A</m> with probability</p>

<p>
<md>0.6</md>
</p>

<p>and type <m>B</m> with probability</p>

<p>
<md>0.4.</md>
</p>

<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X\mid A]=5</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X\mid B]=9.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
=
5(0.6)+9(0.4).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]=6.6.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-conditional-expectation-independence">
<title>Conditional Expectation and Independence</title>
<p>If <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are independent, then conditioning on <m>Y</m> does not
change the distribution of <m>X</m>.</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y]
=
\mathbb{E}[X]
}</md>
</p>

<p>almost surely, provided <m>\mathbb{E}[X]</m> exists.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-law-total-variance">
<title>Law of Total Variance</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Law of Total Variance]
If <m>X</m> has finite variance, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\mathbb{E}
\left[
\operatorname{Var}(X\mid Y)
\right]
+
\operatorname{Var}
\left(
\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y]
\right).
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>This decomposes total variability into:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{average within-group variability}
}</md>
</p>

<p>plus</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{between-group variability}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-total-variance-interpretation">
<title>Interpretation of Total Variance</title>
<p>The first term,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[\operatorname{Var}(X\mid Y)],</md>
</p>

<p>measures uncertainty remaining after <m>Y</m> is known.</p>

<p>The second term,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y]),</md>
</p>

<p>measures how much the conditional means differ across values of <m>Y</m>.</p>

<p>Thus variability can be decomposed according to explanatory structure.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-total-variance">
<title>Worked Example: Variance Decomposition</title>
<example>
<title>Within and Between Variability</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>P(Y=0)=P(Y=1)=\frac12.</md>
</p>

<p>Assume</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y=0]=2,
\qquad
\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y=1]=6,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X\mid Y=0)
=
\operatorname{Var}(X\mid Y=1)
=
3.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[\operatorname{Var}(X\mid Y)]
=
3.</md>
</p>

<p>The conditional mean variable takes values <m>2</m> and <m>6</m> equally
likely, so its mean is <m>4</m> and variance is</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
(2-4)^2+(6-4)^2
}{2}
=
4.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
3+4
=
7.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-higher-moments">
<title>Higher Moments</title>
<p>Higher moments describe aspects of shape beyond location and spread.</p>

<p>The third central moment is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mu_3
=
\mathbb{E}
\left[
(X-\mu)^3
\right].
}</md>
</p>

<p>The fourth central moment is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mu_4
=
\mathbb{E}
\left[
(X-\mu)^4
\right].
}</md>
</p>

<p>These lead to skewness and kurtosis.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-skewness">
<title>Skewness</title>
<definition>
<title>Skewness</title>
<statement>
<p>If the required moments exist and <m>\sigma&gt;0</m>, the standardized
skewness is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\gamma_1
=
\frac{
\mathbb{E}[(X-\mu)^3]
}{
\sigma^3
}.
}</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The Greek letter</p>

<p>
<md>\gamma</md>
</p>

<p>is called <q>gamma.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-skewness-interpretation">
<title>Interpretation of Skewness</title>
<p>Roughly,</p>

<p>
<md>\gamma_1&gt;0</md>
</p>

<p>suggests right-skewness,</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>\gamma_1&lt;0</md>
</p>

<p>suggests left-skewness.</p>

<p>For a distribution symmetric about its mean, when the third moment
exists,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\gamma_1=0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Zero skewness does not necessarily imply symmetry.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-kurtosis">
<title>Kurtosis</title>
<definition>
<title>Kurtosis</title>
<statement>
<p>If the fourth moment exists, kurtosis is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\gamma_2
=
\frac{
\mathbb{E}[(X-\mu)^4]
}{
\sigma^4
}.
}</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>For a normal distribution,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\gamma_2=3.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-excess-kurtosis">
<title>Excess Kurtosis</title>
<p>Excess kurtosis is defined by</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\gamma_2-3.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the normal distribution has excess kurtosis</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Kurtosis is strongly influenced by tail behavior and extreme
observations.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-factorial-moments">
<title>Factorial Moments</title>
<p>For an integer-valued random variable <m>X</m>, the <m>k</m>-th falling
factorial is</p>

<p>
<md>(X)_k
=
X(X-1)\cdots(X-k+1).</md>
</p>

<p>The corresponding factorial moment is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[(X)_k].
}</md>
</p>

<p>Factorial moments are especially useful for count distributions.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-poisson-factorial-moments">
<title>Poisson Factorial Moments</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[(X)_k]
=
\lambda^k.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This simple formula is one reason the Poisson distribution appears
naturally in combinatorial counting problems.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-poisson-second-moment">
<title>Worked Example: Second Poisson Moment</title>
<example>
<title>Using a Factorial Moment</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda),</md>
</p>

<p>we know</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X(X-1)]
=
\lambda^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>X^2
=
X(X-1)+X,</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X^2]
=
\lambda^2+\lambda.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\mathbb{E}[X^2]
-
(\mathbb{E}[X])^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)=\lambda.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-moment-existence">
<title>Moment Existence</title>
<p>A distribution may have some finite moments but not others.</p>

<p>For example, a heavy-tailed distribution may satisfy</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[|X|]&lt;\infty</md>
</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X^2]=\infty.</md>
</p>

<p>Then the mean exists but the variance is infinite.</p>

<p>Thus one should not assume all moments exist.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-cauchy-moments">
<title>The Cauchy Distribution and Moments</title>
<p>For a standard Cauchy variable,</p>

<p>
<md>f_X(x)
=
\frac1{\pi(1+x^2)}.</md>
</p>

<p>The expected value</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]</md>
</p>

<p>does not exist as an absolutely convergent integral.</p>

<p>Its variance also does not exist.</p>

<p>Thus symmetry alone does not guarantee a finite mean.</p>

<p>\begin{warningbox}
A distribution can be perfectly symmetric and still fail to have a
finite expectation.
\end{warningbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-pareto-moment-existence">
<title>Pareto Moment Existence</title>
<p>For a Pareto distribution with shape parameter <m>\alpha</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X^k]</md>
</p>

<p>is finite only when</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
k&lt;\alpha.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore:</p>

<p>
<md>\alpha\leq1</md>
</p>

<p>implies the mean is infinite,</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>\alpha\leq2</md>
</p>

<p>implies the second moment and variance fail to be finite.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-markov-inequality">
<title>Markov's Inequality</title>
<p>Expectations can provide probability bounds without knowing the full
distribution.</p>

<p>\begin{theorem}[Markov's Inequality]
If</p>

<p>
<md>X\geq0</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>a&gt;0,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P(X\geq a)
\leq
\frac{
\mathbb{E}[X]
}{a}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-proof-markov">
<title>Proof of Markov's Inequality</title>
<p>Since <m>X\geq0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>X
\geq
a\mathbf{1}_{\{X\geq a\}}.</md>
</p>

<p>Taking expectations gives</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
\geq
a
\mathbb{E}
\left[
\mathbf{1}_{\{X\geq a\}}
\right].</md>
</p>

<p>Using</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}
\left[
\mathbf{1}_{\{X\geq a\}}
\right]
=
P(X\geq a),</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P(X\geq a)
\leq
\frac{\mathbb{E}[X]}{a}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-markov">
<title>Worked Example: Markov Bound</title>
<example>
<title>Bounding a Large Value</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>X\geq0</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]=10.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>P(X\geq50)
\leq
\frac{10}{50}.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
P(X\geq50)\leq0.2.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-chebyshev-inequality">
<title>Chebyshev's Inequality</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Chebyshev's Inequality]
If <m>X</m> has finite mean <m>\mu</m> and variance <m>\sigma^2</m>, then for
every <m>a&gt;0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P(|X-\mu|\geq a)
\leq
\frac{\sigma^2}{a^2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Setting</p>

<p>
<md>a=k\sigma</md>
</p>

<p>gives</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P(|X-\mu|\geq k\sigma)
\leq
\frac1{k^2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-proof-chebyshev">
<title>Proof of Chebyshev's Inequality</title>
<p>Apply Markov's inequality to the nonnegative random variable</p>

<p>
<md>(X-\mu)^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>P(|X-\mu|\geq a)
=
P((X-\mu)^2\geq a^2).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\leq
\frac{
\mathbb{E}[(X-\mu)^2]
}{
a^2
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Hence,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P(|X-\mu|\geq a)
\leq
\frac{\sigma^2}{a^2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-chebyshev">
<title>Worked Example: Chebyshev Bound</title>
<example>
<title>Within Three Standard Deviations</title>
<p>For any random variable with finite variance,</p>

<p>
<md>P(|X-\mu|\geq3\sigma)
\leq
\frac19.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>P(|X-\mu|&lt;3\sigma)
\geq
1-\frac19.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
P(|X-\mu|&lt;3\sigma)
\geq
\frac89.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-chebyshev-normal-comparison">
<title>Chebyshev Versus the Normal Empirical Rule</title>
<p>Chebyshev's inequality applies to essentially any distribution with
finite variance.</p>

<p>It therefore gives relatively conservative bounds.</p>

<p>For <m>k=2</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>P(|X-\mu|&lt;2\sigma)
\geq
\frac34.</md>
</p>

<p>For a normal distribution, the actual probability is approximately</p>

<p>
<md>0.95.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus distribution-specific information can provide much sharper bounds.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-cantelli-preview">
<title>One-Sided Chebyshev Preview</title>
<p>A useful one-sided refinement is Cantelli's inequality.</p>

<p>For <m>a&gt;0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P(X-\mu\geq a)
\leq
\frac{
\sigma^2
}{
\sigma^2+a^2
}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This can be substantially sharper than applying ordinary Chebyshev to a
one-sided event.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-jensen-preview">
<title>Jensen's Inequality Preview</title>
<p>Expectation also interacts with convexity.</p>

<p>If <m>\varphi</m> is convex and the expectations exist, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\varphi(\mathbb{E}[X])
\leq
\mathbb{E}[\varphi(X)].
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is Jensen's inequality.</p>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi</md>
</p>

<p>is called <q>phi</q> or <q>varphi.</q></p>

<p>Jensen's inequality is developed more fully in analysis and advanced
probability.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-variance-jensen">
<title>Variance from Jensen's Inequality</title>
<p>Take</p>

<p>
<md>\varphi(x)=x^2,</md>
</p>

<p>which is convex.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>(\mathbb{E}[X])^2
\leq
\mathbb{E}[X^2].</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X^2]
-
(\mathbb{E}[X])^2
\geq0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This gives another proof that variance is nonnegative.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-moment-inequalities-preview">
<title>Moment Inequalities</title>
<p>Several major inequalities connect moments:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{Cauchy--Schwarz},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{Jensen},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{Hölder},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{Minkowski}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>These become fundamental tools in measure-theoretic probability and
functional analysis.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-cauchy-schwarz-rv">
<title>Cauchy--Schwarz for Random Variables</title>
<p>If <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> have finite second moments, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|\mathbb{E}[XY]|
\leq
\sqrt{
\mathbb{E}[X^2]
\mathbb{E}[Y^2]
}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Applying this to centered variables gives</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)|
\leq
\sigma_X\sigma_Y.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This proves the correlation bound.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-standardization-expectation">
<title>Standardization</title>
<p>For a random variable with finite mean <m>\mu</m> and positive standard
deviation <m>\sigma</m>, define</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
Z
=
\frac{X-\mu}{\sigma}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[Z]=0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(Z)=1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus standardization centers and rescales a random variable.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-standardization-general">
<title>Worked Example: Standardizing an Arbitrary Variable</title>
<example>
<title>Mean Zero and Variance One</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]=12</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X)=25.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\sigma_X=5.</md>
</p>

<p>Define</p>

<p>
<md>Z
=
\frac{X-12}{5}.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[Z]=0,
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(Z)=1.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-expectation-pgf-preview">
<title>Expectation and Probability Generating Functions</title>
<p>For a nonnegative integer-valued random variable with PGF</p>

<p>
<md>G_X(s)
=
\mathbb{E}[s^X],</md>
</p>

<p>differentiation gives moment information.</p>

<p>Under appropriate conditions,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X'(1)
=
\mathbb{E}[X].
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G_X''(1)
=
\mathbb{E}[X(X-1)].
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus PGFs naturally encode factorial moments.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-mgf-preview-expectation">
<title>Moment Generating Functions Preview</title>
<p>The moment generating function is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X(t)
=
\mathbb{E}[e^{tX}].
}</md>
</p>

<p>When it exists in a neighborhood of <m>t=0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M_X^{(k)}(0)
=
\mathbb{E}[X^k].
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus derivatives of the MGF generate raw moments.</p>

<p>This is developed systematically in Section <xref ref="sec-probability-transforms" />.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-cumulants-preview">
<title>Cumulants Preview</title>
<p>The cumulant generating function is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
K_X(t)
=
\ln M_X(t).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its derivatives at zero are called cumulants.</p>

<p>The first cumulant is the mean:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_1
=
\mathbb{E}[X].
}</md>
</p>

<p>The second is the variance:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa_2
=
\operatorname{Var}(X).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Cumulants are especially useful for sums of independent random
variables.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-expectation-center-mass">
<title>Expectation as Center of Mass</title>
<p>A discrete probability distribution can be interpreted as point masses.</p>

<p>Place mass</p>

<p>
<md>p_X(x)</md>
</p>

<p>at each location <m>x</m>.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
}</md>
</p>

<p>is the center of mass.</p>

<p>For continuous distributions, the density plays the role of distributed
mass.</p>

<p>This geometric interpretation explains many expectation formulas.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-variance-inertia">
<title>Variance as Moment of Inertia Analogy</title>
<p>Variance measures squared distance from the center.</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\mathbb{E}[(X-\mu)^2]
}</md>
</p>

<p>is analogous to a one-dimensional moment of inertia around the center
<m>\mu</m>.</p>

<p>Far-away probability receives much more weight because deviations are
squared.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-expectation-fair-games">
<title>Expectation in Gambling and Fair Games</title>
<p>Suppose a game produces net payoff <m>X</m>.</p>

<p>The game is often called fair if</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]=0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>A positive expected payoff favors the player in the long-run average
sense.</p>

<p>A negative expected payoff favors the opposing side.</p>

<p>Expected value alone does not measure risk.</p>

<p>Variance and the full distribution are also important.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-worked-game-payoff">
<title>Worked Example: Expected Game Payoff</title>
<example>
<title>A Simple Game</title>
<p>Suppose a player wins \$10 with probability</p>

<p>
<md>0.2</md>
</p>

<p>and loses \$3 with probability</p>

<p>
<md>0.8.</md>
</p>

<p>Let <m>X</m> be net payoff.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
=
10(0.2)
+
(-3)(0.8).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>=
2-2.4.</md>
</p>

<p>The expected net loss is \$0.40 per play.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
-0.4.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-expectation-long-run">
<title>Expectation and Long-Run Behavior</title>
<p>Expected value is connected to long-run averages through laws of large
numbers.</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>X_1,X_2,\ldots</md>
</p>

<p>are suitable repeated observations with mean <m>\mu</m>, then the sample
average</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
X_1+\cdots+X_n
}{n}</md>
</p>

<p>tends toward <m>\mu</m> in an appropriate probabilistic sense.</p>

<p>This is developed later in the chapter.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-expectation-moments-common-errors">
<title>Common Errors</title>
<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-expected-value-must-be-a-possible-outcome">
<title>Assuming Expected Value Must Be a Possible Outcome</title>
<p>Expectation is a weighted average.</p>

<p>It need not belong to the support.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-probability-weights">
<title>Forgetting Probability Weights</title>
<p>For a discrete random variable,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X]
\neq
\sum_x x.</md>
</p>

<p>The correct expression is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbb{E}[X]
=
\sum_xxp_X(x).
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-expectation-always-exists">
<title>Assuming Expectation Always Exists</title>
<p>Heavy-tailed distributions may have undefined or infinite moments.</p>

<p>Always check convergence when necessary.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-variance-is-e-x-2">
<title>Assuming Variance Is <m>\mathbb{E}[X^2]</m></title>
<p>The correct formula is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(X)
=
\mathbb{E}[X^2]
-
(\mathbb{E}[X])^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-to-square-scale-factors-in-variance">
<title>Forgetting to Square Scale Factors in Variance</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>Y=aX+b,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{Var}(Y)
=
a^2\operatorname{Var}(X).
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-adding-variances-without-checking-covariance">
<title>Adding Variances Without Checking Covariance</title>
<p>In general,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Var}(X+Y)
\neq
\operatorname{Var}(X)
+
\operatorname{Var}(Y).</md>
</p>

<p>The covariance term must be included unless it is zero.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-zero-covariance-means-independence">
<title>Assuming Zero Covariance Means Independence</title>
<p>Zero covariance only rules out linear association.</p>

<p>Nonlinear dependence may remain.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-independence-is-needed-for-linearity-of-expectation">
<title>Assuming Independence Is Needed for Linearity of Expectation</title>
<p>Linearity of expectation does not require independence.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="id-08-probability-06-expectation-moments-subsubsec-confusing-variance-and-standard-deviation">
<title>Confusing Variance and Standard Deviation</title>
<p>Variance has squared units.</p>

<p>Standard deviation has the same units as the random variable.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-conditional-mean-with-marginal-mean">
<title>Confusing Conditional Mean with Marginal Mean</title>
<p>The value</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbb{E}[X\mid Y=y]</md>
</p>

<p>depends on the conditioning value <m>y</m>.</p>

<p>The unconditional mean averages these conditional means over the
distribution of <m>Y</m>.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="id-08-probability-06-expectation-moments-subsubsec-interpreting-correlation-as-causation">
<title>Interpreting Correlation as Causation</title>
<p>Correlation describes statistical association.</p>

<p>It does not by itself establish a causal relationship.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-small-variance-means-small-mean">
<title>Assuming Small Variance Means Small Mean</title>
<p>Mean and variance describe different features.</p>

<p>A distribution can have a large mean and small variance, or vice versa.</p>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-expectation-moments-subsec-expectation-moments-applications">
<title>Applications</title>
<p>\begin{applicationbox}</p>

<p><em>Statistics.</em>
Means, variances, covariances, and higher moments form the foundation of
estimation, sampling theory, regression, and statistical inference.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Finance.</em>
Expected return, variance, covariance matrices, and correlation are
central to portfolio and risk models.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Engineering.</em>
Expected load, expected lifetime, uncertainty, and reliability are
summarized using moments.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Operations Research.</em>
Expected demand, waiting time, cost, and service requirements guide
decision models.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Combinatorics.</em>
Indicator variables and linearity of expectation provide powerful
methods for counting random structures.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Machine Learning.</em>
Means and covariance matrices summarize distributions and appear in
Gaussian models, optimization, and dimensionality reduction.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Physics.</em>
Expectation values, fluctuations, and covariance describe uncertain
physical quantities.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Biology.</em>
Means, variances, and correlations summarize biological measurements,
population variation, and experimental outcomes.</p>

<p>\end{applicationbox}</p>
</subsection>

